{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["<b>And it was told the king of Egypt that the people fled</b> — He sent escorts with them, and when the three days that they had set to go and return had arrived and they saw that they were not returning to Egypt, they went back and told Pharaoh on the fourth day; on the fifth and sixth they pursued after them; on the night of the seventh they went down into the sea; in the morning they said the song, and that is the seventh day of Passover — therefore we read the Song on the seventh day. This is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Exodus 14:5), and so it is in the Mekhilta (ad locum). And by way of the plain sense, it is as HaShem spoke and Pharaoh said to the children of Israel: for when the children of Israel did thus — they turned back and encamped before Pi-hahiroth before Baal-zephon — this was told to the king of Egypt, and he said that the people had fled and they are entangled in the wilderness and are not going to a known place to sacrifice. And this is the reason for \"and the children of Israel went out with a high hand\" (Exodus 14:8): that they made for themselves a banner and standard to be raised, and they went out with rejoicing and with songs, with timbrel and lyre, in the manner of those redeemed from slavery to freedom, not like slaves about to return to their labor — and all this was told to him."], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 14, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:16:53.477740408Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}